 "exercise": {
  "id": "pqp03-ex05",
  "chapter": 3,
  "number": "3.5",
  "title": "Equal up to a number",
  "level": 2,
  "statement": "The proof claims $2\\cdot f = f$, and the checker says ✗.\n\n- (a) Read the checker's message (hover the ✗). Change the step's relation from = to ≈. What does ≈ claim (PQP Def. 3.39)?\n- (b) Change it back to =, and make the claim true by adding a **number** node (the Scalar in the toolbar) to D₂. A label that reads as a number — 2, 1/2, 0.5, 1/√2, −i — is that number; any other label is a symbol.\n- (c) Now change the 2 in D₁ to 0. Why does neither = nor ≈ hold any more, and why does Def. 3.39 insist that the numbers be non-zero?",
  "done": "After (b) the proof shows ✓ with =, and you can answer (c).",
  "book": "textbook/ch03.html#ex-3-5"
 }
